    {
      "meaning": "Grace and God's Torah",
      "description": "The number five is linked to God's grace and His teaching. The Torah consists of five books (the Pentateuch). David's five stones against Goliath and the five loaves in the feeding miracle are connected with God's care and equipping. In the tabernacle, the number five was consistent in the measurements.",
      "reason": "[minor] The description is somewhat brief and could usefully elaborate a bit better on the connection between grace, Torah, and the concrete examples. But as a whole, it is theologically correct.; [minor] Interesting details about the specific measurements of the tabernacle involving the number five (for example, five pillars at the entrance, five cubits high for the hangings of the courtyard) would have fitted well as a footnote to enrich the text without breaking the flow.; [minor] The Hebrew word for five (חָמֵשׁ, chamesh) and its possible connection to other Hebrew words could have been mentioned in a linguistic footnote.; [minor] The Meaning field says 'Grace and God's Torah'. Although this is a common association, it could be formulated somewhat more precisely, for example 'Grace, God's Torah, and equipping' to better capture the breadth of the symbolism.",
      "score": 6,
      "date": "2026-04-04"
    }
  ],
  "footnotes": [
    {
      "text": "The Hebrew word for five is חָמֵשׁ (chamesh). Some scholars have pointed to a possible connection to the root ח-מ-שׁ, which can also mean 'armed' or 'equipped', strengthening the link between five and divine equipping.",
      "source": "lingvistisk"
    },
    {
      "text": "The tabernacle's use of the number five appears in several details: the entrance to the Holy Place had five pillars of acacia wood (Exod 26:37), the altar of burnt offering was five cubits long and five cubits wide (Exod 27:1), and the courtyard hangings were five cubits high (Exod 27:18).",
      "source": "arkeologisk"
    },
    {
      "text": "In rabbinic tradition, the five books of Moses (Chumash, from chamesh – five) are regarded as God's complete teaching to Moses, and the number five is therefore associated with complete divine instruction.",
      "source": "rabbinsk"
    }
  ]
